description:
Build a Fenwick tree and a segment tree, and use the Fenwick tree to count inversions:

```cpp
class Fenwick
{
public:
    explicit Fenwick(std::size_t n);         // n zeros; positions 0 .. n-1
    void add(std::size_t i, long long delta);
    long long prefix(std::size_t count) const;          // a[0] + ... + a[count-1]; prefix(0) is 0
    long long sum(std::size_t l, std::size_t r) const;  // a[l] + ... + a[r], inclusive
    std::size_t lowerBound(long long target) const;     // smallest count with prefix(count) >= target (all values >= 0)
};

class MaxTree
{
public:
    explicit MaxTree(const std::vector<int>& a);
    void set(std::size_t i, int value);
    int max(std::size_t l, std::size_t r) const;        // inclusive; std::out_of_range if l > r or r >= size
};

long long countInversionsFenwick(const std::vector<int>& a);   // O(n log n) using a Fenwick tree
```

- `Fenwick` uses the lowest-set-bit trick internally (1-based inside, 0-based outside). `lowerBound` assumes no value is negative and returns `n + 1` if even the whole sum is below `target`; `lowerBound(0)` is 0. It must be O(log n): walk down the powers of two ("binary lifting") rather than binary searching over `prefix`.
- `MaxTree` is a segment tree for range maximum.
- `countInversionsFenwick` replaces each value by its rank among the sorted distinct values (coordinate compression), then walks from right to left asking how many smaller values were already seen.

